tachInstance) so I can wire individual tabs? Detaching loses reuse. Alternative: wire reactions on the TabBar component's internal tab frames once — but destination differs? No! Destinations are the same for every screen: Home→03, Analytics→09, Cards→07, Profile→08. So define reactions INSIDE the component once — instances inherit prototype interactions! Yes, instance children inherit reactions from component children in Figma prototypes. \n\nBut reactions inside component referencing destination frames: fine.\n\nHowever, there's a catch: when a prototype interaction is on a component child and you click it in an instance, it works. Yes.\n\nSimilarly, header back buttons: destinations differ per screen, so wire per screen on the back icon node directly (screen child, not instance child).\n\nAlso for states: active tab differs per screen — instance overrides on icon/label colors are fine.\n\nSo TabBar component: 4 tab frames named Tab_Home etc., each with icon + label, all inactive gray by default; per screen override active one to PRIMARY_DEEP. And reactions set inside component. But careful: setting reactions inside component with destination = frames created later — I'll add reactions at the end (stage Z) when all frames exist, targeting the component's children.\n\nSimilarly \"View switch\" (My Balance / Upcoming Bills / Transactions segmented control) appears on 03/04/05 with destinations → same targets from each: 03→04,05; 04→03,05; 05→03,04. Same destinations regardless of current screen, so component-level reactions work if I make ViewSwitch a component with 3 segment frames wired to 03/04/05. \n\nAccount header (avatar, Hi Esaaa!, messages, notifications) on 03/04/05: messages → 06. Notifications → nothing (no screen). Make it a component too with message icon wired to 06.\n\nAlright. Let me also plan components: \"Screen/Header\" variants differ — build per screen manually.\n\nNow the amount of code: big but manageable. Let me carefully write screen builder